The **resource curse** , also known as the **paradox of plenty** or the
**poverty paradox** , is the phenomenon of countries with an abundance of [
natural resources ](/wiki/Natural_resource "Natural resource") (such as [
fossil fuels ](/wiki/Fossil_fuel "Fossil fuel") and certain [ minerals
](/wiki/Mineral "Mineral") ) having less [ economic growth
](/wiki/Economic_growth "Economic growth") , less [ democracy
](/wiki/Democracy "Democracy") , or worse [ development
](/wiki/Economic_development "Economic development") outcomes than countries
with fewer natural resources.  [1]  There are many theories and much academic
debate about the reasons for and exceptions to the adverse outcomes. Most
experts believe the resource curse is not universal or inevitable but affects
certain types of countries or regions under certain conditions.  [2]  [3]

